1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,094
For a long time, people didn't believe plates move.

2
00:00:03,094 --> 00:00:07,972
One clue: the coasts of South America and Africa fit together like jigsaw pieces.

3
00:00:07,972 --> 00:00:12,613
Scientists suggested there was once a single giant continent that broke apart.

4
00:00:13,672 --> 00:00:19,443
Volcanoes and earthquakes occur mainly along plate boundaries, such as the Pacific Ring of Fire,

5
00:00:19,443 --> 00:00:20,871
where plates are moving.

6
00:00:21,931 --> 00:00:27,821
Identical fossils of Mesosaurus, a freshwater reptile, are found in both South America and Africa.

7
00:00:27,821 --> 00:00:32,521
It couldn't have swum across an ocean, so the lands must once have been joined.

8
00:00:33,581 --> 00:00:39,114
At the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, rising magma cools into rock that records Earth's magnetic field,

9
00:00:39,114 --> 00:00:40,898
which has flipped many times.

10
00:00:40,898 --> 00:00:45,896
Matching stripes on both sides show the plates moving apart, 2.5 centimetres a year.
